%I A398260 #13 Jul 31 2026 01:05:09
%S A398260 0,0,1,0,2,0,2,2,1,6,0,5,0,2,6,5,4,0,5,3,0,3,2,9,0,4,9,3,6,14,4,5,13,
%T A398260 5,2,12,1,28,0,14,9,14,11,6,15,11,9,6,4,18,0,12,17,0,3,27,14,8,0,5,26,
%U A398260 9,15,3,9,3,2,32,20,10,11,34,20,14,25,5,16,29,28,2
%N A398260 a(1)=0; for n>=2, let s = product of the decimal digits of a(n-1); if s=a(m) for some m < n-1, let m be the greatest such index and set a(n) = n-1-m; otherwise set a(n) = 0.
%C A398260 Van Eck-like sequence using the product of the decimal digits of the previous term as lookup key.
%C A398260 The sequence agrees with A181391 through a(30)=14. The first difference occurs at a(31). See example.
%C A398260 By contrast, A181391 looks up the value 14 itself, which had not appeared previously, yielding a(31)=0.
%C A398260 Every lookup key is a 7-smooth number. Indeed, any decimal digit product has all prime factors contained in {2,3,5,7}. Consequently, numbers such as 11, 13, 17, 19, 22, ... can never occur as lookup keys.
%C A398260 Among the first 10^6 terms there are 165 zeros; the last observed zero occurs at index 954584.
%C A398260 Among the first 10^6 terms, only 562 distinct lookup-key values occur.
%C A398260 Numerical evidence suggests long-term behavior substantially different from both A181391 and the digit-sum Van Eck variant A398259. No simple asymptotic law is currently known.
%e A398260 a(30)=14, and the product of the decimal digits of 14 is 4. The most recent earlier occurrence of 4 is a(26)=4, hence a(31) = 30 - 26 = 4.
%o A398260 (Python)
%o A398260 from math import prod
%o A398260 def A398260_list(n):
%o A398260     if n <= 0:
%o A398260         return []
%o A398260     seq = [0]
%o A398260     last = {}
%o A398260     for ii in range(n - 1):
%o A398260         key = prod(map(int, str(seq[ii])))
%o A398260         seq.append(ii - last[key] if key in last else 0)
%o A398260         last[seq[ii]] = ii
%o A398260     return seq
%Y A398260 Cf. A007954, A181391, A398259.
%K A398260 nonn,base,easy,new
%O A398260 1,5
